.TH OB-FP-DAEMON 8 "September 2026" "open-bastion" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
ob-fp-daemon \- privileged sink for the SSH key fingerprint spool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ob-fp-daemon
.PP
Not run directly. Activated per-deposit by
.BR systemd (1)
through
.B ob-fp.socket
(\fI/run/open-bastion/ssh-fp.sock\fR).
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pam_openbastion
needs to know which SSH key authenticated a session, and OpenSSH does not
export
.B SSH_USER_AUTH
to the PAM environment during
.BR pam_acct_mgmt .
The
.B AuthorizedPrincipalsCommand
helper
.RB ( ob-ssh-principals )
therefore records the fingerprint out of band, in
\fI/run/open-bastion/ssh\-fp/<anchor>.fp\fR, where \fI<anchor>\fR is the pid of
the per-connection
.B sshd-session
monitor.
.PP
sshd requires
.B AuthorizedPrincipalsCommandUser
to be unprivileged, so before issue #249 that helper wrote the spool itself and
the directory was \fB0700 nobody\fR. The integrity of the whole fingerprint
binding then rested on
.BR nobody :
a shared, low-trust account that several daemons run under. Code execution as
that user could read every deposited fingerprint and write a well-formed drop
at any pid.
.PP
.B ob-fp-daemon
takes the deposit instead. It runs as root, keeps the spool \fB0700 root\fR,
and writes the drops itself, so nothing unprivileged can create, list or read
anything there.
.SH WHAT THE SOCKET CHECKS
.SS The depositing user
.BR SO_PEERCRED ,
which the kernel fills in at
.BR connect (2)
time and no client can forge, must be the owner of the listening socket \(em or
root.
.PP
That owner comes from
.B SocketUser
in
.IR ob-fp.socket ,
which ships as \fBnobody\fR to match the
.B AuthorizedPrincipalsCommandUser
both setup scripts configure. There is no configuration key for it: the socket
unit is the single place the answer is written, so an administrator who changes
the principals user and overrides
.B SocketUser
in a drop-in gets a daemon that follows rather than one that locks them out.
.PP
With
.B SocketMode=0600
the kernel already refuses anyone else, so this check is defence in depth \(em
it keeps holding if a drop-in loosens the mode.
.SS The session, which is derived and not received
This is the check that carries the weight. The anchor pid is derived from the
.B depositing process's own
.I /proc
ancestry \(em it is never taken from the request. A client therefore cannot
name the session it deposits for.
.PP
To place a drop on a given anchor you must already be a descendant of that
anchor, and the anchor must be a live
.B sshd-session
monitor whose
.B real
uid is root. The effective uid is not used: sshd switches it to the helper user
while the principals command runs, which is when the deposit happens. sshd puts exactly one unprivileged thing in that position: the
principals helper. A daemon started from init descends from pid 1, not from an
.BR sshd-session ,
and cannot re-parent itself into one; a logged-in user's shell is under an
.B sshd-session
but runs as the user, which the uid check rejects.
.PP
Forging a binding therefore requires code execution as the helper user
.I inside the target connection's own process tree
\(em strictly smaller than "code execution as nobody anywhere on the host",
which was enough before #249.
.SH PROTOCOL
Three newline-delimited lines on the connected socket, at most 20480 bytes:
.TP
.B line 1
the fingerprint, \fBSHA256:<base64>\fR (required)
.TP
.B line 2
the key algorithm, sshd's \fB%t\fR (may be empty)
.TP
.B line 3
the key blob, sshd's \fB%k\fR in base64 (may be empty)
.PP
The reply is one line, \fBOK\fR or \fBERR <reason>\fR.
.PP
A malformed algorithm or blob costs the
.B .key
metadata drop but never the fingerprint: the
.B .fp
drop is what the LLNG binding needs, and losing it would remove a security
control over a cosmetic input error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/open-bastion/ssh-fp.sock
The activation socket. \fB0600\fR, owned by the principals helper user.
.TP
.I /run/open-bastion/ssh-fp/
The spool. \fB0700 root:root\fR; the daemon re-asserts owner and mode on every
deposit, so a host upgraded from the pre-#249 \fBnobody\fR-owned directory is
migrated on first use rather than left on the old trust root while appearing
fixed.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Every refusal is logged to
.B LOG_AUTHPRIV
and returned to the caller, which sshd writes to the authentication log. If the
socket is not enabled the helper cannot deposit at all: logins still succeed,
but with no fingerprint binding \(em
.B pam_openbastion
reports the missing drop (issue #192), and
.BR ob-bastion-setup (8)
or
.BR ob-backend-setup (8)
enables
.B ob-fp.socket
when re-run.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ob-fp-submit (8),
.BR ob-cert-daemon (8),
.BR ob-bastion-setup (8),
.BR ob-backend-setup (8),
.BR pam_openbastion (8)
.SH AUTHOR
Linagora
